  "text": "Question seven, the behavioral one. How do you use AI tools in your day-to-day work? A vague answer, like I use Copilot a lot, scores poorly. Give a concrete workflow. What you hand to the assistant: boilerplate, test scaffolding, unfamiliar APIs, first drafts. What you don't: security-sensitive code, and the core design. How you verify what it writes. And one specific time it was wrong, and how you caught it. It also helps to show that you measure rather than assume. In meter's twenty twenty-five randomized trial, experienced open-source developers took nineteen percent longer on tasks where AI was allowed, while estimating afterward that it had made them about twenty percent faster. Meter has since said that result reflects early twenty twenty-five tools, and its later data points toward a speedup. The lesson that lasts is the size of the gap between how fast a tool feels and how fast it is.\n\nEvery round in this series comes back to the same test: telling a good answer from a plausible one, and saying why. An assistant can produce either. Telling them apart is still your job. Every topic page on the track has concept checks, most have code you can run, and a bank of a hundred and ten multiple-choice questions covers the whole track.",
